// Formative practice quiz for props. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/components-and-state/props

::props o1 - default value::A child is declared as function Price with destructured props amount and currency, with currency defaulting to the text USD. The parent passes only amount. What is currency inside the child?{
~undefined#Not quite. Without the default it would be undefined, but a default was declared.
~An empty string#Not quite. React does not fill in empty strings for missing props.
~React throws an error#Not quite. Missing props are allowed. They are simply undefined unless a default is given.
=USD#Correct. A missing prop takes its destructuring default.
}

::props o1 - non-string prop::You want to pass the number 3 as a count prop so the child can do arithmetic with it. How do you write it?{
~count\="3", with quotes#Not quite. Quotes pass the string 3, so adding 1 would produce the text 31.
=count\=\{3\}, with braces#Correct. Braces pass a JavaScript value, here the number 3.
~count\=3, bare#Not quite. JSX attribute values need quotes or braces.
~number count\=3#Not quite. That is not JSX syntax.
}

::props o2 - where children go::A Card wrapper is used with a paragraph nested inside its tag, but the paragraph never appears on screen. What is the most likely cause?{
=Card does not place the children prop in its returned JSX#Correct. Nested JSX arrives as children, and it only shows where the wrapper renders it.
~Paragraphs cannot be passed as children#Not quite. Any JSX can be nested, including paragraphs.
~Card must first copy children into state#Not quite. children is an ordinary prop and can be rendered directly.
~Nested content needs a key prop#Not quite. Keys are for items in a list, not for ordinary nesting.
}

::props o3 - read-only props::A child needs to display an updated value after a click. Where should that value change?{
~In the child, by assigning to the prop#Not quite. Assigning to a prop breaks the rule that props are read-only to the child.
~In the child, by editing the props object directly#Not quite. The props object is read-only for the child, for the same reason.
=In the parent that owns it, which then renders the child with the new prop#Correct. The owner changes the value and the child receives it on its next render.
~Nowhere, because props can never change#Not quite. Props do change over time. A parent can pass new values on a later render.
}
